In comp.os.linux.alpha  DiskMan <nobody@thewheel.com> wrote:
> I was wondering, is there a way to compile Samba using Compaq's Compiler
> (CCC) instead of GCC?

It looks like, from what you are quoting, that a few trivial edits
should be enough.

> I keep receiving the following errors:
....
> cc: Error: /usr/include/glob.h, line 201: In this declaration, the type of
> "glob64" is not compatible with the type of a previous declaration of
> "glob64" at line number 186 in file /usr/include/glob.h. (notcompat)

Fire up an editor, find out why 'ccc' complains about "not compatible"
(maybe some gcc extension was used? maybe ccc has standard conformance
bugs?) and make it compatible.  Actually this is a system header file
and it looks like that either you have your sources misconfigured
('./configure' botched a job?) or you are missing some header file.
If your 'glob.h' is the same as the one I have on hand then line 186 is
behind
#if _FILE_OFFSET_BITS != 64 || __GNUC__ < 2
so 'ccc' should not see it at all unless it has a buggy preprocessor
(some versions of 'ccc' had this problem, IIRC).  OTOH line 201 is
behind
#ifdef _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
which does not really makes sense on Alpha and also seem to contradict
the previous define.  Hence it looks like that you have a self-inflicted
mess which likely can be untangled with a correct use of ./configure.

Your compiler is pretty specific about what it does not like but without
looking at the code it is hard to tell.  Samba sources usually were
rather portable and standard conforming.

  Michal


