In comp.os.linux.alpha  DiskMan <nobody@thewheel.com> wrote:
> Well I checked with Samba.org and Build.Samba.org... it doesn't compile with
> CCC...

Sigh!  We already know that it does not compile "out-of-the-box" because
you already told us.

> Thanks for your help...

You would do better if you would follow advice to find where is your
editor.

As far as I can see samba compiles just fine with 'ccc' after minor
adjustments.  One of trivial ways to do that is to make in 'source'
directory a private copy of /usr/include/glob.h and apply to it
the following patch:

--- /usr/include/glob.h	Fri Mar 21 15:44:10 2003
+++ glob.h	Sat Mar 29 17:32:35 2003
@@ -137,6 +137,7 @@
 #endif
   } glob_t;
 
+#undef _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
 #ifdef _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
 # ifdef _GNU_SOURCE
 struct stat64;

As I wrote before there is really no point in _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
as on Alpha file offsets are 64-bit anyway.

After that you can do
'CC=ccc CFLAGS="whatever" ./configure <all_configure_options_you_desire'
and 'make'.  There is a lot of warnings caused by DEBUG string
constants, so they likely can be turned off, but other than that nothing
special.  Oh, I actually see also some "ptrmismatch" warnings so they
could be in sources genuine bugs but this is a different question from
"can I compile".  Yes, you can, at least with sources as given
in the current update to RH 7.2-alpha - samba-2.2.7-2.7.2.src.rpm.
Most likely any other version too.

I guess that the first thing can be classified as a bug in 'ccc' which
should provide its own 'glob.h' instead of doing blindly 
#include_next <glob.h>
This can be a more thorough fix on your private installation.

Cleaner ways to resolve this tiny hiccup are left as an exercise
to the reader.  Results may even work despite these pointer problems
as apparently gcc compiled binaries do not bomb although this may
be a dumb luck in a memory layout.  I cannot check.  I do not do windows.

  Michal


