 DiskMan <nobody@thewheel.com> wrote:
> Hmmm, I tried what you said but it didn't work. Infact I checked my SRM
> console and jez, it doesn't even show anything installed for a NIC.

If this is a PCI network card then it should show up in SRM as eva0,
IIRC.  I cannot check in this moment.  There are variables in SRM
which allow to set NIC as "autonegotiate" or some fixed speed.
Depending on what is on the other end of a wire you are hooked up to
assorted card may not be able to autonegotiate.  This depends both
on a hardware and software and this is really the problem with some
of many variants of tulips.

If your SRM truly cannot see the card then maybe there is an SRM
update for your board?

> Infact mii stated that
> the driver didn't support it and provided me with a nice indescript error
> message [;-)

Sounds like not exactly a moderately recent 3com card. :-)  Maybe it
simly does not operate hardware-wise at more speeds than one?

What is printed in 'dmesg' after you inserted a driver module, or when
your kernel detected a card if you have that built-in, may tell you
more.  Some NIC drivers may have various extra arguments allowing you to
"tune" that card or at least to try that.  It is much easier to
experiment with such things if you are using a driver as a module
because you can take an interface down, 'rmmod' that module, and restart
the whole thing with modified arguments in /etc/modules.conf.  No reboot
required.  Check 'Documentation' subdirectory of kernel sources.

  Michal


