steven <sir_funzone@antispam.yahoo.com> wrote:

Misguided quoting "jeopardy style" makes reasonable responding quite
difficult.  Don't do that!

> How would you fix this problem in SRM,

There is nothing to fix in SRM.

> I am having the same troubles as this
> person.

No, you don't (and this is explained in installation instructions).

> I am able to get the server up but that is if I go into aboot
> and then select the correct kernal.

It is spelled "kernel".  Assuming a standard installation you
have a choice of one so it better be correct. :-)

As it was written on this list a number of times 'aboot' loader
can handle 'initrd' images, which may contain system specific
drivers, while this is not exactly the case with (officially
unsupported) 'milo' boot loader and that is why a person you
refer to had troubles requiring an extra hacking.

At SRM prompt type

   b <your_boot_device_here> -fl 0

and it should boot.  The later because an installer hopefuly
wrote a correct boot command in your 'aboot.conf' (which you
can list from 'aboot> ' prompt by typing there 'l'; and you can
see other aboot commands following instructions displayed on your
screen).

If installer somehow did screw up and the above does not work
then explain what really happened and indeed ask for help if
you have a hard time figuring that yourself.  In particular
post then, precisely, what is printed on your screen after
'l' command from aboot.

If you are already at 'aboot> ' prompt then type '0' there,
or one of other defined labels, and effects will be the same
as with a boot command above.

> I would like to be able to reboot this
> thing without going through this hassle since I will not be the only one
> working on the box.

By setting correctly in SRM variables 'bootdef_dev', 'boot_flags' (to
0), and 'auto_action' to BOOT you can make your box to boot on power-up
(but check spelling of these variables using 'show' as this is from
memory and it may be wrong if close enough).  Later changes in such
automatic boot sequence, because - for example - you replaced your
kernel with a different version, are done by editing 'aboot.conf'.

  Michal


